16:10:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: You’ve hit on a really interesting question that mixes physics with human biology. The short answer is: the tiny amount of water vapor in steam doesn’t condense into a pool of liquid in your lungs because your airways are already warm and wet, and any condensation that does occur is microscopic and instantly absorbed—far too little to cause choking. Let’s break that down. ### 1. Steam isn’t really “steam” – it’s mostly hot, humid air What we call “steam” in a shower or sauna is actually a mist of extremely tiny liquid water droplets suspended in air (like a cloud) or simply very humid air at temperatures typically between 40–60 °C (104–140 °F), not true dry steam at 100 °C. Either way, the total mass of water in that air is surprisingly low. Even saturated air at 50 °C holds only about 83 grams of water per cubic meter. A normal human breath is about 0.5 liters (0.0005 m³), so one inhalation brings in roughly **0.04 grams** of water vapor. That’s about one single drop of water. Even if every bit of that vapor condensed into liquid, it would be a microscopically thin film spread across the huge surface area of your lungs—about 70 m², the size of a tennis court. You wouldn’t even notice it. ### 2. Your airways are already at 100% humidity The inside of your respiratory tract, from the nose down to the alveoli, is always lined with a thin layer of mucus and fluid. The air there is fully saturated with water vapor at body temperature (37 °C). When you inhale hot, humid air, it immediately begins to cool to body temperature. Because cooler air can’t hold as much moisture, water vapor will tend to condense out—**onto the already wet surfaces of your airways**. This is exactly the same thing that happens when you breathe dry winter air: your nose adds moisture and warmth to protect the delicate lung tissue. In the case of steam, your airways are just doing the reverse, cooling and dehumidifying the inhaled air slightly. The conden