16:11:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That sounds like a genuinely scary experience, and I can see why you’d connect the dots to water literally building up—your body was sending you a strong “can’t breathe” alarm. What you felt was very real, but it almost certainly wasn’t liquid water filling your lungs like a drowning scenario. Instead, a few different things were likely happening at once, all of which can create that terrifying sensation of chest tightness and suffocation. Let's break down what probably went on, because your body’s reaction makes perfect sense when you see the full picture. ### 1. The steam itself can be an irritant, especially if it’s too hot or impure In a steam room, the air is often much hotter than your body is used to—potentially 45–55°C (113–131°F) or even higher near the steam outlet. Inhaling very hot, moisture-laden air can be a direct irritant to your airway linings, like a mild thermal burn. Your body’s reflex to protect the delicate lower airways is to instantly trigger a cough and make you feel like you need to clear something out. That “filling up” sensation is often swelling and irritation, not liquid. Also, steam rooms aren’t sterile. If the water used to generate steam has any chemicals (chlorine from tap water, cleaning residues, or even minerals), those become concentrated airborne irritants. They can cause immediate bronchial inflammation, making your airways feel raw and tight. ### 2. Bronchoconstriction – your airways clamp down This is a big one. For many people, breathing hot, humid air can trigger a reflex narrowing of the bronchial tubes (bronchospasm), similar to an asthma attack. This is especially common if you have any underlying airway sensitivity—even mild, undiagnosed asthma, or seasonal allergies. The smooth muscle around your small airways suddenly cramps, making it physically harder to move air in and out. Your brain interprets that resistance as “my lungs are clogged with something.” You cough and feel like you’re drowning, when in reality the pipes themselves have shrunk, not filled with water. The same reflex can happen with rapidly changing humidity. If you went from a cool, dry area straight into a dense steam cloud, your airways can freak out at the sudden shift. ### 3. Excess mucus production – a rapid response Your airways are lined with mucus-producing cells. When they’re hit with intense steam, they can go into overdrive, pumping out a lot of thin, watery mucus in a short time to protect themselves. This can instantly give you the sensation of “gunk” in your chest that you need to cough up. Combined with the cough reflex and some bronchospasm, it feels exactly like you’re filling up with fluid. But that mucus is your own body’s secretion, not condensed steam, and it’s actually a protective, self-cleaning response. You likely coughed up some phlegm afterwards, which further reinforced the idea that it was the steam “condensing.” ### 4. Laryngospasm – your vocal cords slam shut If you inhaled a particularly hot or intense blast, a reflex called laryngospasm can occur: your vocal cords in the larynx suddenly and forcefully close to prevent anything (real or perceived) from entering the lungs. This cuts off airflow almost completely for a few seconds. It’s terrifying—you can’t breathe, can’t speak, and you feel an intense pressure in your throat/chest. This is the same reflex that causes “dry drowning” after a gulp of water down the wrong pipe. Once it relaxes, you gasp and cough violently. Many people describe it exactly as you did: feeling like something was filling up their chest and they were choking. ### So, what was the “water” you felt? It was a combination of: - **Swollen, inflamed airway tissue** (feels thick and clogged) - **Suddenly increased, thin mucus** (feels like fluid) - **Airway constriction** (feels like a blockage) - Possibly a brief spasm of your vocal cords, then the desperate gasp. At no point was room-steam actually pooling in your lungs. Even if you