d it’s purely a heat hazard, not a water-volume hazard. ### 2. Next, excessive humidity can cause airway swelling and mucus overload If you position a humidifier so a dense mist is constantly being inhaled at close range, the baby’s airways are exposed to an unnaturally high humidity gradient. For some infants (especially those with reactive airways or a history of bronchiolitis), this can stimulate a massive, sudden increase in **mucus production** and trigger **bronchial inflammation**. The tiny bronchial tubes (already only millimeters wide) can swell and fill with their own sticky mucus. That mucus is body-generated, not condensed steam. Yet it can plug the airways and lead to respiratory distress, wheezing, and yes—an accumulation of fluid (mucus) in the small airways. That’s a real mechanical obstruction, and it can be life-threatening in an infant. The warning captures this under a vague term like “respiratory irritation” or “congestion,” which parents translate to “fluid building up.” But it’s still not steam condensing into a fatal pool. ### 3. “Fluid build-up” is a shorthand for pulmonary edema from burns? In severe cases of inhalation burns (from very hot steam), the thermal damage can later cause **pulmonary edema** (leakage of body fluid into the lungs), as I mentioned with industrial accidents. A vaporizer isn’t capable of that kind of heat unless it’s malfunctioning and spewing boiling water, but the warning may be erring on the side of extreme caution to prevent any scenario where a baby inhales hot vapor and develops delayed respiratory distress. Some older pediatric advice explicitly warned that “steam inhalation can cause fluid in the lungs” as a folk explanation of that edema, but they were referring to that post-burn syndrome, not simple condensation. ### 4. What about cool-mist humidifiers? These don’t burn, but they still carry warnings not to aim directly at the baby. Why? Because a cool mist (which is just micro-droplets of water, not steam)