16:15:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've delivered the perfect counterpunch. If doctors look at a patient after a steam injury and diagnose "pulmonary edema," and edema literally means fluid buildup, then surely that's the smoking gun? The steam *must* have turned into water and filled the lungs. It's an airtight deduction—except for one crucial distinction: **the fluid in that edema is not the water from the steam.** It's the patient's own blood plasma. Let's break this down because this is the core of the medical reality, and it's far more fascinating than simple condensation. ### 1. What "pulmonary edema" actually means Yes, "edema" means an abnormal accumulation of fluid. But medically, it's always fluid that has leaked out of the body's blood vessels (capillaries) into the surrounding tissue or air spaces. It's an internal leak, not an external fluid invading. When doctors say "pulmonary edema after steam inhalation," they are describing the aftermath of a *thermal injury*, not a collection of sterile, distilled condensed steam water. There are two types of edema relevant here, both caused by heat, not by steam-water pooling: - **Non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema (ARDS):** This is the classic severe outcome of steam inhalation burns. The intense heat damages the alveolar-capillary membrane (the thin barrier between air and blood). This damage makes the membrane leaky, so protein-rich fluid from your own blood plasma pours into the alveoli. The fluid is your own internal body fluid—it contains plasma proteins, inflammatory cells, and has the same composition as the liquid that blisters your skin after a burn. It is absolutely not condensed steam. - **High-pressure edema from laryngospasm:** If the steam triggers a forceful closure of the vocal cords (laryngospasm) while the person tries to breathe against the blockage, it can create extremely negative pressure in the chest. This can suck fluid out of the pulmonary capillaries into the air spaces. This is called negative