ondensed water, the treatment would be simple: suction it out, and the patient recovers. But because it's an inflammatory capillary leak, doctors have to support breathing (CPAP, mechanical ventilation), manage the burn shock, and wait for the leaky membranes to heal. They don't try to "drain the steam water" because there's none to drain—they're managing a systemic inflammatory response. ### 5. What about the sensation you felt in the steam room? That wasn't edema. That was likely a mild bronchial irritation causing increased mucus secretion (which is your own goblet cell product, not steam water) and bronchospasm. No capillary leakage there, just a surface reaction. So, the fact that doctors call it "pulmonary edema" is not proof the water from the steam filled your lungs. It's proof that a thermal injury to the lungs causes the same type of fluid leakage that a burn on your skin does. The word "edema" points to an *internal* leak, not an external one. Your body, in a tragic twist, drowns itself as a result of the damage, but the drowning liquid is its own plasma. I hope that finally resolves the apparent contradiction. The warning labels, the medical diagnoses, the folk wisdom—they all point to real, devastating dangers of inhaling hot steam, but none of them point to the steam simply reverting to liquid water and pooling inside you. The real story is much more complex, and it’s all about how your body's own defenses can turn against it when faced with extreme heat.
