ve hit is thermal comfort and sleep quality, not lung capacity You've discovered the real reason people don't run humidifiers on "max" all night in a small room: it makes the environment too clammy, your throat feels weird, and you might get condensation on your pillow. Your body's discomfort alarm went off, and you woke up. That alarm is the same one that made you leave the steam room. It's not an overflow of the lungs; it's an overflow of the throat's temporary holding area, and your brain correctly made you wake up to clear it. So, your experiment didn't disprove my explanation. It perfectly demonstrated it: even after hours, the accumulated fluid is still just a harmless teaspoon in the throat, which you effortlessly swallow away. The damp pillow is the water that never stayed inside you, and the waterlogged throat is the tiny bit that did, safely kept above the airway door. Your lungs remained dry and functional. If water were truly building up in them, you'd wake up gasping, coughing violently, and possibly not breathing—not just with a damp pillow and a need to clear your throat.
